---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="Sebastian Correa-Gallego">
  <header class="hero">
    <img
      class="hero__img"
      src="/assets/field.webp"
      alt="Sebastian Correa-Gallego taking samples by headlamp in the Organal San Antonio, a volcaniclastic cave in Támesis, Colombia."
      fetchpriority="high"
    />
    <div class="hero__scrim" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="hero__inner">
      <div class="hero__content">
        <h1 class="hero__name">Sebastian<br />Correa-Gallego</h1>
        <p class="hero__degree">B.Sc. in Biology</p>
        <p class="hero__phrase">
          On how microbial communities assemble — and whether the state they reach is
          predictable from the cells themselves.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <a class="hero__cue" href="#research" aria-label="Continue to research"><span aria-hidden="true"></span></a>
  </header>

  <section class="focus" id="research">
    <div class="wrap focus__inner">
      <h2 class="focus__lead reveal">
        How does order arise from parts that were previously autonomous — and does that
        process follow rules, or depend on history?
      </h2>

      <div class="focus__grid">
        <div class="focus__text reveal">
          <p>
            The question is most tractable in microbial community assembly, where one pool of
            organisms may converge on a single reproducible state or settle into any of several,
            depending on which arrives first. Ecology describes both outcomes; no general rule yet
            says which one a given community will take.
          </p>
          <p>
            I ask whether that can be anticipated from the organisms themselves — from how a cell
            divides its finite proteome between growing quickly and growing efficiently. Organisms
            alike in that division should exclude one another early and converge; a pool spanning
            the whole rate–yield axis should cross-feed instead, and dependence is what admits
            several outcomes. Allocation is measurable, which would make the shape of that
            landscape a quantity rather than a caveat — and the same question continues upward,
            toward the thresholds at which cooperating parts cease to be separable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="focus__figure reveal">
          <svg class="fig" viewBox="0 0 340 192" role="img" aria-labelledby="fig-title">
            <title id="fig-title">
              Two stability landscapes. Where a species pool spans a narrow range of allocation
              strategies the landscape has a single basin and assembly converges on one state;
              where it spans a wide range the landscape has two basins, and which one a community
              settles in depends on the order its members arrive.
            </title>

